<article class="env-exercise" id="02B3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02B3">Exercise <span data-tag="02B3">111.52.8</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Consider the rings </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  A &  = k[x, y]/(xy) \\  B &  = k[u, v]/(uv) \\  C &  = k[t, t^{-1}] \times k[s, s^{-1}] \end{align*}
</div>
  <p> and the $k$-algebra maps </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \begin{matrix}  A \longrightarrow C, 

&  x \mapsto (t, 0), 

&  y \mapsto (0, s) 

\\  B \longrightarrow C, 

&  u \mapsto (t^{-1}, 0), 

&  v \mapsto (0, s^{-1}) 

\end{matrix}  \]
</div>
  <p> It is a true fact that these maps induce isomorphisms $A_{x + y} \to C$ and $B_{u + v} \to C$. Hence the maps $A \to C$ and $B \to C$ identify $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(C)$ with open subsets of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B)$. Let $X$ be the scheme obtained by glueing $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B)$ along $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(C)$: </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \amalg _{\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(C)} \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B).  \]
</div>
  <p> As we saw in the course such a scheme exists and there are affine opens $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \subset X$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B) \subset X$ whose overlap is exactly $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(C)$ identified with an open of each of these using the maps above. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Why is $X$ separated? 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Why is $X$ of finite type over $k$? 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Compute $H^1(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$, or what is its dimension? 
</p>
</li><li><p>
What is a more geometric way to describe $X$? 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>